  "source": "Vision read of ALL 14 of the 14 flyer pages meta.json lists (ad_pages 14, on_disk 14), installed by pull-bakers.ps1 on 2026-09-09 with the OMAHA and CURRENT gates both verified against store label 'Pickup at Saddlecreek | 888 S Saddle Creek Rd'. WINDOW TAKEN FROM THE AD'S OWN PRINTED FOOTER on page 1, 'SALE DATES: Wed. September 9 - Tues. September 15, 2026', which agrees with ad-schedule.json and with meta.json. Page 8 is a pharmacy flu-shot ad and carries no priced items; every other page contributed rows. WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED, AND THE RULE USED. Same-item multibuys are recorded VERBATIM ('2/$5', '6/$9', 'Buy 1, Get 1 Free') because Get-ItemPrice in pricing-math-lib.ps1 derives a real per-item price from them and buying N of ONE commodity is a price a shopper can actually reach. CROSS-CATEGORY BASKET OFFERS ARE NOT: the pages 4-5 'buy more save more' panels take $1 off each item only when 5 or more DIFFERENT participating items are bought in one transaction, and the engine has no handler for that condition - a plain '$1.99' would be read as the shelf price and would UNDERSTATE Baker's on 26 rows. Those items are therefore recorded at their UNCONDITIONAL with-card price (the '2.99 with card' number printed above the offer price), which is what one unit actually costs. The same rule excluded the price-less offers entirely: 'SAVE $5 when you spend $20 on produce' (p1), 'SAVE $10 an extra when you buy 2 participating household items' (p7), 'Olly SPEND $20 SAVE $5' and 'Command SAVE 20%' (p9), 'Huggies SAVE $5/ea' (p3) - a discount is not a unit price and none of them states one. Beer carrying a PayPal cash-back rebate (p7, p13) is recorded at the TILL price, not the post-rebate price, because a rebate is refunded after purchase and is not what the register charges; digital-coupon items ARE recorded at the coupon price because the coupon reduces the till price. A BOGO whose only stated regular is a ceiling ('regular retail up to $5.99 lb') carries regular=null rather than the ceiling: it is an upper bound, not a regular, and the engine correctly leaves such a row unpriced instead of pricing it off a fabricated number.",
